<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Taras</h1><p class="st-meta">706 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.) · capital Taras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The only Spartan colony in Italy, which grew into the leading Greek city of southern Italy and fought Rome with the help of Pyrrhus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition says that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partheniai">Partheniai</a>, sons of Spartan women born during the Messenian War, were sent out under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanthus">Phalanthus</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> in 706 BCE. The city occupied a peninsula between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mar-piccolo">Mar Piccolo</a> lagoon and the open sea, giving it the best harbour on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> grew rich on fishing, purple dye, fine wool, horses and pottery; Apulian red-figure vases were made there. After a heavy defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iapygians">Iapygians</a> around 473 BCE it became a democracy. In the 4th century BCE the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archytas">Archytas</a>, a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, was repeatedly elected general. Taras led the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italiote-league">Italiote League</a> and hired Greek commanders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archidamus-iii">Archidamus III</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander of Epirus</a>, against Italic peoples.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> began in 282 BCE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentines</a> attacked a Roman squadron. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a> came to their aid, but after his departure Taras surrendered in 272 BCE. It defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in 212 BCE and was sacked when retaken in 209 BCE. Roman Tarentum survives as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-taranto">Taranto</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 706 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.65%;width:8.64%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>706 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Archytas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tarentum, Taranto (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-489&amp;focus=taras" aria-label="Taras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5104 732 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taras lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5424 988L5300 825M5424 988L5304 942M5424 988L5410 996"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 825h0M5304 942h0M5410 996h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5424 988h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5424 988h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Taras. <a href="/atlas?year=-489&amp;focus=taras">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

